## The Locale File

The system locale is a list of shell-style assignments, such as `LANG=en_US.UTF-8`, that login sessions, `systemd` and `pam_env` read at startup. Distributions keep it in one of two files:

| File | Used by |
|------|---------|
| `/etc/locale.conf` | systemd-based distributions: Fedora, RHEL and its rebuilds, Arch, openSUSE |
| `/etc/default/locale` | Debian, Ubuntu |

Unless `path` is set, the file is detected when the resource is created: `/etc/default/locale` if it is a regular file, else `/etc/locale.conf` if it exists (on recent Ubuntu releases `/etc/default/locale` is a symlink to it), else `/etc/default/locale` if `/etc/debian_version` exists, else `/etc/locale.conf`. The detected path is recorded in state and kept afterwards. Setting `path` to another file replaces the resource; the old file is left as it is, or restored with `restore_on_destroy`.

The resource owns `LANG` and every `LC_*` variable in the file:

- `LANG` is set to `lang`, and the variables in `lc` to their values. An existing assignment is rewritten in place if its value differs, and left exactly as it is, including quotes, if it doesn't. Missing ones are appended, `LANG` first.
- Every other `LC_*` assignment, including `LC_ALL`, and repeated assignments of a variable, are removed, so that the file sets exactly what the configuration says.
- Everything else, such as comments and `LANGUAGE`, is kept.

Assignments are read the way a shell reads them: with or without `export`, and with the value in double quotes, single quotes or none. Values are written without quotes; locale names are validated to contain only letters, digits, `_`, `.`, `@`, `+` and `-`, so they never need any.

The file is written atomically, keeping its mode, owner, ACLs and SELinux label, and edits are [serialised](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/blechschmidt/sysutils/latest/docs#concurrency-and-locking) with other resources of the provider. A missing file is created with mode `0644`, together with its directory. A symlink at `path` is refused. Nothing is written if the file already assigns the configured values. With the provider's [`root_dir`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/blechschmidt/sysutils/latest/docs#root-directory) set, `path` and the detection are inside the root.

The new locale applies to sessions and services started afterwards. `systemd-localed` notices the change the next time it reads the file; `localectl status` may show the old value until then.

## Generating Locales

A locale must be compiled before programs can use it; until then they fall back to `C`. When applying, the provider lists the installed locales with `locale -a` and compares them with `lang` and the values of `lc`, ignoring how the codeset is spelled (`en_US.UTF-8` and `en_US.utf8` are the same). `C` and `POSIX` are always installed.

- With `generate = false` (the default), a missing locale is reported as a warning and the file is written anyway.
- With `generate = true`, missing locales are compiled:
  - If `/etc/locale.gen` and `locale-gen` exist (Debian, Ubuntu, Arch), each locale is looked up in `/usr/share/i18n/SUPPORTED` for its character map and enabled in `/etc/locale.gen`, by uncommenting its line or appending one, and `locale-gen` runs. These `locale-gen` scripts rebuild exactly the locales listed there, so a locale compiled any other way would be deleted by the next run, for example on a package upgrade. On Debian, install the `locales` package first.
  - Otherwise `localedef -i <language> -f <charmap> <locale>` compiles it, as on Fedora and RHEL. The character map comes from the codeset in the name (`UTF-8` for `.UTF-8` or `.utf8`) or from `SUPPORTED`. `localedef` needs the locale sources in `/usr/share/i18n/locales`, which Fedora and RHEL ship in `glibc-locale-source`; installing the language's `glibc-langpack-<language>` package with [`sysutils_package`](./package.md) is the usual alternative there.
  - The locale must be listed by `locale -a` afterwards, or apply fails.
- On musl-based systems such as Alpine there is no `locale` command and nothing to compile; the check is skipped, and `generate = true` fails.
- `generate = true` is refused with `root_dir`, since the locale tools act on the running host.

Generating runs only when a locale is missing, so the check costs one `locale -a` per apply. Compiled locales are not removed on destroy.

## Drift Detection

On refresh, the provider reads `LANG` and the `LC_*` variables from the file. If `LANG` was changed, an `LC_*` variable was added, changed or removed, or the file was deleted, the actual values are recorded and the plan shows an update back to the configuration. Whether the locales are still installed is not checked on refresh.

## Destroy and `restore_on_destroy`

By default destroy leaves the locale as it is.

With `restore_on_destroy = true`, destroy puts back the `LANG` and `LC_*` assignments the file had when the resource was created, recorded in the resource's private state before anything was changed, and removes the file if it did not exist and holds nothing but comments now. Other lines of the file are left as they are. An imported resource has no recorded previous locale; with `restore_on_destroy = true`, its destroy leaves the locale in place and warns.

## Import

There is one system locale, so the import ID is always `system`:

{{ codefile "shell" .ImportFile }}

The locale file is detected as described above, and import fails if it does not set `LANG`. `generate` and `restore_on_destroy` are `false` after import.

## Caveats

- There is one system locale per host, or per `root_dir`. Two `sysutils_locale` resources for the same file fight over it, and so do `sysutils_locale` and a [`sysutils_file_line`](./file_line.md) or [`sysutils_ini_value`](./ini_value.md) editing `LANG` or `LC_*` in it.
- `LANGUAGE` and `LC_ALL` can't be set. `LC_ALL` overrides every category and is meant for one-off use; systemd refuses it in `locale.conf`.
- `localectl` is not used, so the console keymap and X11 layout, which `localectl` also manages, are left alone.
- The resource only works on Unix-like systems.
